To answer that question, I can say that you should've gone back and
reread the arguments more closely.  That wasn't the only argument, nor
was it the primary one.

> Potion alchemy was _added to the game_ to be a
> source of ridiculously many hitpoints and wishes,
> nurse dancing and foocubi dalliance allow ridiculous
> values to show up on status lines as well, and they
> both were _added_ with that intention.

They were most certainly not added with the intention of allowing
"ridiculous" values to show up.

Potion alchemy consumes resources; you must have N potions of extra
healing and one potion of gain energy in order to make N potions of full
healing.  Plus, you must bless them; another resource.

Nurses disappear in relatively short order.

Foocubi develop severe headaches and come with their own associated set
of risks.

> "Monty Haul" is a firmly embedded part of the
> dungeon crawl player mentality, it isn't going to go
> away if pudding farming gets nerfed.

The question is not whether someone is willing to put the time and
effort in; it's whether it's possible to do so with the expenditure of
only time, and not effort.

Pudding farming allows one to put forth minimal effort for infinite
gain.  This is inelegant, broken, and frankly, stupid.

> On the open and public servers, even ascending won't
> guarantee you a place in the saved record file
> despite it being 2000 entries long instead of 100
> entries long as on a personal computer.

That is at least partly due to scoring being broken.  But fixing scoring
is a side issue at this point; there's no point in trying to perform any
elaborate fix to score when it requires a trivial amount of effort to
approach MAXINT by just farming.

So to answer the question: I would like to fix scoring as well, yes, and
make it meaningful for ascensions as well as non-ascensions.

> If pudding farming is suboptimal play anyway, and
> Rider farming is a much less painful way to roll the
> score counter for the *Big Score* addicts, why does
> pudding farming in particular need to come out of
> the game?

Because Rider farming occurs at a point in the game in which the player
has already effectively won, and thus can't make any further use of the
infinite items/etc. _other_ than as score.

I'd like to see Rider farming fixed too, or for that matter any method
of infinitely killing the same monster over and over and over again;
extinctionism is a valid game goal, but it should be enforced
everywhere, not loopholed in certain areas.  But I consider Rider
farming to be less of an immediate problem than pudding farming, if for
nothing else than Rider farming doesn't have the potential to
inconvenience other players... and far fewer players are capable of
Rider farming in the first place.

> Just because some few, vociferous players find it
> offensive, should it go, when it seems many more
> players find it a fun, if occasional, diversion from
> normal game play?

Just because many more players find it offensive, should it go, when it
seems some few, vociferous players find it a fun, if occasional,
diversion from normal game play?

(See how both those paragraphs were meaningless?)

> Players who _want_ to build huge stacks of death
> drops will _find_ a way, even if it is playing
> extinctionist. 

If extinction were honored everywhere monsters could be created,
building huge stacks of death drops would not be a problem in the
slightest.  There is _also_ no good reason that someone should be able
to gain an infinite supply of certain monster types on their quest
levels, for that matter, and yes, that too should be fixed.

In any case, though, you really do need to go back and reread all my
posts.  Most of your questions were answered there, and you've really
completely misunderstood my primary objections to farming, choosing
instead to focus on the fringe.

Since you're a non-ascender and thus don't really have a good
perspective on the game, I'm not sure you'll properly understand, but
that's not my problem; you should maybe sit down and try to win
sometime, and you might get a better perspective on what the issues
really are.

